    "thinking": "We are given the current source code for Problem B: Submatrix of Maximum Sum. The goal is to improve memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking. We have a history of experiments with role A, focusing on these aspects. The selected parent (72eca49f47b44ead) had hypothesis about flattening matrix into 1D array, two-pass integral image, and row-blocking search. That experiment achieved mean_seconds ~0.0625, score ~0.6828.\n\nWe have own role history showing recent experiments: \n- 08422dc8de5640f3 (score 0.6915, time 0.0600) - likely a good candidate.\n- a69e622158bd4a3d (score 0.6913, time 0.0601)\n- 3359e6233a234b02 (score 0.6865, time 0.06145)\n\nThe best so far is around 0.06 seconds.\n\nWe need to propose a new experiment that improves upon these. The current source code already uses flattened matrix, two-pass integral image with H and II, row blocking with B=64. We can consider:\n\n- Tuning block size: maybe smaller or larger? But we have seen experiments with different tile sizes (e.g., 32 vs 48). Possibly try a different block size like 128 or 256 to reduce loop overhead.\n- Loop interchange in search: currently outer loop over i0 blocks, inner over j. Could swap loops so that for each column block we iterate rows? But row blocking already does that.\n- Use of precomputed term_j_s is unused (line \"long long term_j_s = ...\" but not used). That's dead code; remove it to avoid unnecessary computation and memory access.\n- Maybe combine the two passes into a single pass for integral image? Some experiments tried eliminating H matrix. But that might increase complexity or reduce locality.\n- Use of int vs long long: mat is int, II is long long. Could use int for II if sums fit in 32-bit? Problem says result fits 32-bit signed integer, but intermediate sums may exceed? Actually submatrix sum can be up to N^2 * max value (max absolute maybe large). But with N<8000 and values unspecified, could overflow 32-bit. So long long is safe.\n- Use