hat we iterate over columns first, but that might be worse.\n\nBut let's think about memory access pattern in search:\nFor each block of rows (i0 to i_end), and for each column j, we compute sub_sum using II at four points: (i+s,j+s), (i,j+s), (i+s,j), (i,j). These are all within the same row block. The accesses to II are strided by n+1 across rows but contiguous within a row. By blocking rows, we reuse the same column indices for multiple i in the block. That's good.\n\nIf we also block columns, we could further improve temporal locality of II values across j iterations? But note that for each (i,j) we access four elements: two at column j and two at column j+s. If s is large, these are far apart. Column blocking might help if we process a range of j together so that the same row segments are reused.\n\nBut implementing 2D tiling would require more complex loops. However, it could be beneficial.\n\nGiven time, I'll propose:\n- Remove dead code.\n- Increase block size to 128 (or maybe 256) to reduce loop overhead and improve cache line utilization for II rows within a block.\n- Possibly also change the inner loop order: instead of iterating i from i0 to i_end inside j loop, we could iterate over columns first? Actually current is fine.\n\nBut note that in the search loop, we have:\nfor (int i0 = 0; i0 <= n - s; i0 += B) {\n    int i_end = std::min(i0 + B, n - s + 1);\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n        // precompute term_j_s (unused)\n        for (int i = i0; i < i_end; ++i) {\n            sub_sum = ...\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nThis is row-major order: for each column, we iterate over rows in the block. That's good because II accesses are contiguous within a row? Actually inside inner loop, we access II at (i+s,j+s), etc. For fixed j, as i increases, these indices increase by n+1 per step, which is strided but still sequential if we consider memory layout of II: rows stored contiguously. So accessing II[i*(n+1)+j] for increasing i will access every (n+1)th element, causing cache misses because each row is large. However, within a block of 64 rows, the stride is n+1 (~8000), so we are jumping across rows but still within a small range? Actually if n=8000, then II has 8001 columns per row, so each row is ~64KB (8 bytes * 8001 ≈ 64008 bytes). L1 cache maybe 32-64KB. So accessing one element from different rows will evict previous lines. But because we are iterating over i in the inner loop for a fixed j, we access II[i*(n+1)+j] and II[(i+s)*(n+1)+j], etc. These accesses have stride n+1 (8001) which is large, so each iteration likely misses cache. However, within a block of 64 rows, the total number of distinct rows accessed is at most 2*block_size? Actually we access i and i+s for each i in [i0,i_end). So if s is small relative to n, then i+s may be outside the block but still close. But overall, this pattern has poor spatial locality because consecutive iterations jump by large stride.\n\nA better approach might be to swap loops: iterate over rows first (outer loop) and columns inner? That would give contiguous access within a row for each column j, which is good. For example:\nfor (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n        sub_sum = II[(i+s)*(n+1)+(j+s)] - ...;\n    }\n}\nThis accesses II in row-major order: as j increases, we access consecutive elements within the same row. That's excellent spatial locality. And for each i, we also need rows i and i+s, but those are separate rows. However, this pattern is exactly what the baseline sliding window does? Actually original algorithm uses a sliding window to compute sums in O(SN^2). But using integral image gives O(N^2) with constant time per submatrix.\n\nThe current row-blocking approach tries to reuse column indices across multiple i values, but due to large stride it may not help much. Perhaps the best is to use simple double loop over i and j without blocking, because that yields contiguous memory access for II along columns (j dimension). Let's test: if we do:\nfor (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\n    const long long* row_i = &II[i*(n+1)];\n    const long long* row_is = &II[(i+s)*(n+1)];\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n        sub_sum = row_is[j+s] - row_i[j+s] - row_is[j] + row_i[j];\n    }\n}\nThis accesses row_i and row_is sequentially, which is cache-friendly. The inner loop over j has stride 1 for each of the four arrays (though they are separate pointers). This should be very efficient.\n\nBut we need to consider that II is large; accessing two rows at a time may still cause capacity misses if n is large. However, it's better than strided access across rows.\n\nGiven our best experiments achieved ~0.06s with row blocking, maybe the simple double loop without blocking could be even faster? Let's check history: experiment 08422dc8de5640f3 (score 0.6915) - we don't know its source code but it might have used a different approach.\n\nWe can propose to replace the search loops with a straightforward i-j nested loop that accesses II in row-major order, removing blocking and dead code. This simplifies code and improves spatial locality for column access.\n\nAlso, we could consider using pointer arithmetic instead of indexing to reduce overhead.\n\nThus transformations:\n- Remove unused variable term_j_s.\n- Replace blocked search with simple double loop over i then j (row-major).\n- Use pointers to rows for faster access.\n\nLet's write the new code accordingly. Also keep integral image construction as is (two-pass). Possibly we can also combine H and II into a single pass? But that might be more complex; let's focus on search phase.\n\nHypothesis: \"Replacing row-blocked search with a straightforward double loop over rows then columns improves spatial locality because the inner loop accesses consecutive elements within each integral image row, maximizing cache line utilization. Removing dead code eliminates unnecessary memory loads.\"\n\nWe'll set block size to something else? Act